## Exact claim and boundaries

Theorem 1 states that, for **every fixed** (H\in(1/4,1)), two independent standard fractional Brownian components with that common Hurst parameter, every (T>0), every integer (n\ge1), and **every** square-integrable estimator (F\) measurable with respect to both complete uniform grids,

\[
\|X_T-F\|_2\ge c_H T^{2H}n^{1/2-2H},\qquad
X_T=\int_0^T B_s^1\,dB_s^2.
\]

The target is the canonical **iterated** integral, defined through the (L^2) polygonal limit. Observations are (B^i_{jT/n}) for (i=1,2), (j=1,\ldots,n): (n) observations per component, including (T). The deterministic initial values are zero. Nonlinear estimators are included; conditional expectation is the minimum-error instance.

The constant is the displayed explicit positive H-only expression (2\pi a/[D_H\sqrt{\overline P_f\overline P_g}]), with (a=4/45045), the specified harmonic normalization integral, and the specified periodization bounds. The actual cell regularity in the body is (W^{3,1}) for zero-extended piecewise polynomials. The abstract's adjective “smooth” is not a claim of infinite differentiability.

This is an inequality for each (n,T), including (n=1). The note does not assert a sharp constant, an exact normalized-error limit, a limiting law, H-uniform positivity, either endpoint, nonuniform or adaptive sampling, unequal Hurst parameters, correlated components, or arbitrary Gaussian linear information. It must not be advertised as a new upper-bound theorem or as a new smooth-range asymptotic rate.

The paper distinguishes the target from antisymmetric conventions. Subtracting the observed endpoint product (B_T^1B_T^2/2) gives the halved antisymmetric area and preserves the optimal conditional residual. The unhalved antisymmetric difference has twice that residual. Those factors must remain visible in any public explanation.

`claim-boundary-map.json` records the full hypothesis list, boundary cases, and six proof interfaces: established area construction; sufficient left-sum convergence; normalized Gaussian covariance paths; upper Gram control by periodization; independence of the quadratic witness from the whole observation vector; and the exact deterministic pairing followed by Cauchy–Schwarz. Their mathematical validity is explicitly left to the separate review lanes.

